<Base title="Documentation">
  <PageIntro eyebrow="Documentation" title="Enough to try the idea in real work.">Dispatch runs locally, keeps agent processes alive in tmux, and uses the tools already present in a repository. Installation retrieves a released Bun binary; a production host does not need Node or pnpm to run Dispatch.</PageIntro>
  <article class="reading">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <p>Dispatch needs PostgreSQL, tmux, GitHub CLI, and at least one supported coding-agent CLI. Start from a checkout, run the preflight check, then install the launchd service.</p>
    <pre class="diagram">git clone git@github.com:selfcontained/dispatch.git
cd dispatch
bin/preflight
bin/install-launchd</pre>
    <p>The installer clones its managed checkout to <code>~/.dispatch/server</code>, downloads the latest released <code>dispatch-release.tar.gz</code>, extracts the platform-matching compiled Bun binary, and registers it as a launchd service. Building from source is only a fallback when a release artifact is unavailable.</p>
    <h2>Three concepts to begin with</h2>
    <section class="principle"><span class="number">01</span><div><h3>Durable sessions</h3><p>Agents run in tmux so their work can survive a browser tab, a network interruption, or the time between two decisions.</p></div></section>
    <section class="principle"><span class="number">02</span><div><h3>Isolated changes</h3><p>Worktrees let an agent investigate or implement a change without borrowing the working directory another person is using.</p></div></section>
    <section class="principle"><span class="number">03</span><div><h3>Shared context</h3><p>Messages, pins, media, reviews, and agent events preserve the information that should outlast a terminal transcript.</p></div></section>
    <p>For implementation details, read the <a href="/architecture">architecture overview</a> and the repository’s <a href="https://github.com/selfcontained/dispatch">README ↗</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
